{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Who</b> — Some say that <b>ha-magbiḥi</b> (who exalts) is an intransitive participle, like \"if you exalt [yourself] like an eagle.\" The truth is that it is a transitive participle and the object is \"your canopy.\" R. Moses said: the object of <b>ha-magbiḥi</b> is His glory; the yod of <b>ha-magbiḥi</b> is like the yod of <b>ḥotzbi marom</b> (the workings of the height), and he said that thus the meaning is: <b>ha-magbiḥi</b> — to sit in the heavens; <b>ha-mashpili</b> — to look upon the earth. And similar to it: \"shoham stones for the ephod\" and \"filling stones for the breastpiece.\" And in my opinion: it is as it sounds." "<b>Ha-magbiḥi</b> — the place of His sitting." "<b>And in the heavens and on the earth</b> — all are lowly before Him. And the meaning of \"to look\" is that He knows everything, like a thing that is seen; and this is what is written in the Torah: \"He is the God of gods\" and after it: \"who executes justice for orphan.\""], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 113, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The word קנך ('your canopy') appears in the biblical text of Psalms 113:6, not verse 5; Ibn Ezra introduces it here as the grammatical object of the participle. I read א\"ר משה as a citing formula ('said R. Moses'); it could alternatively be an abbreviation for אמר רבי משה.", 2 "The closing colon after גר יתום is retained literally from the source; the biblical verse (Deut. 10:17-18) continues with additional items in its full form."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:39:41.567867567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}